    "body": "Concurrent Cython invocations sharing a compiler-cache directory can write the same temporary filename, causing cache publication to fail. Abandoned temporary files accumulate outside the cache quota, and transient cache filesystem errors can turn an otherwise valid compilation into a build failure.\n\nCoordinate cache access with a shared lifecycle lock and per-entry writer locks, using `flock` on POSIX and `LockFileEx` on Windows. Publish complete artifacts through unique temporary files and `os.replace()`, checking for an existing entry under its write lock. Store temporary artifacts in a separate directory so cleanup can reclaim interrupted writes without deleting older Cython writers' active temporary files.\n\nCleanup acquires the lifecycle lock exclusively without waiting for active cache operations, and coalesces requests within one second across processes. Reuse one artifact-directory scan for size accounting, LRU eviction, and orphaned entry-lock reclamation. Replace the `du` subprocess with portable `os.scandir()` accounting that excludes lock metadata.\n\nTreat cache-specific filesystem and locking failures as cache misses or skipped cache writes, while preserving errors writing the requested generated output. Add process/thread concurrency and filesystem-failure regression coverage, and document shared-cache behavior.\n\n",
